# Project rules for resume_ai

## Response style
Be terse. Show the diff, not a narration of the diff. No summary section at the end
unless the change spans more than three files.

## Environment
- Python >=3.12, dependencies managed with `uv` (`uv.lock` is authoritative; `poetry.lock`
  is legacy — do not update it).
- Run tests with `uv run pytest`. Run the app with `uv run gunicorn`.
- Deployed to k3s as the `resume-ai` Deployment. Config comes from the `resume-ai-secrets`
  Secret via `envFrom`, not from a mounted `.env`.

## LLM backend — read this before touching any model code
- Never `from langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI`. Always
  `from src.resume_bot.llm import ChatOpenAI`. That module is a provider factory that
  returns either the OpenAI client or `ChatOllama`, selected by `LLM_PROVIDER`. It keeps
  the OpenAI-shaped call sites unchanged; bypassing it silently pins a file to OpenAI.
- Production runs `LLM_PROVIDER=ollama` against a self-hosted model. OpenAI is the
  fallback path only.
- Do not cache a chat model in a module-level global. `asyncio.run()` opens and closes a
  fresh event loop per call, and a cached `ChatOllama` holds an async client bound to the
  loop that has already closed — the second request dies with
  `RuntimeError: Event loop is closed`. Build the model inside the function.
- `langchain*` is pinned to `<0.4.0`. `langchain-ollama` 1.x is incompatible with that
  pin. Do not bump either without checking the other.

## LangGraph state
- Every key in `AgentState` that more than one tool can write MUST declare a reducer
  (`Annotated[T, fn]`). Local models parallelise tool calls far more readily than GPT-4
  did; two tools writing one key in a single step raises `InvalidUpdateError`, which
  poisons the checkpoint permanently — the thread then 500s even on read.
- Conversations are checkpointed in SQLite. Back the file up before any migration or
  repair.

## Resume tailoring — correctness rules
The tailoring pipeline edits a real person's real resume. Fabrication is the primary
failure mode, not a style issue.

- On local backends the edit plan vocabulary is deliberately limited to `REORDER:` and
  `BOLD:`. `REPLACE:` was removed on purpose — it made fabrication structurally
  impossible rather than merely discouraged. Do not add it back.
- Content validation runs at a 0.985 similarity threshold under `strict_content=True`.
  A looser threshold (0.70 was tried) lets partial fabrication through: swapping a few
  words of a bullet for phrasing lifted from the job ad still scores ~0.8.
- LaTeX escapes are load-bearing. `\textasciitilde10` must not become `~10` — the latter
  renders as a non-breaking space, silently dropping the word "approximately" from a
  quantified claim. `\%`, `\&`, `\_`, `\#` likewise.
- New `\href` URLs are only legitimate if they came in via `relevant_projects`.

## Kubernetes
- Probe timeouts must exceed the slowest realistic request. Generation can take minutes;
  a 1s `timeoutSeconds` will kill the pod mid-request and look like a crash loop.
- Never mount a hostPath over `/app` — it shadows the application code baked into the
  image.
